=== SIGNATURE ===
is_unique(arr, [consec])

=== COMMENT ===
1 if every non-blank value in arr is distinct (case-insensitive), else 0. With consec = 1 the values must ALSO be integers that form one unbroken run in any order (7,5,6 -> 1; 1,2,4 -> 0; 1,2,2 -> 0); consec is 0 by default.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(Arr, [consec], LET(
   chk, z_opt(0, consec),
   flatArr, TOCOL(Arr, 1, 4),
   filtArr, FILTER(flatArr, flatArr <> ""),
   rowCount, ROWS(filtArr),
   matchMatrix, --(filtArr = TRANSPOSE(filtArr)),
   freq, MMULT(matchMatrix, SEQUENCE(rowCount, 1, 1, 0)),
   uniq, IF(MAX(freq) > 1, 0, 1),
   IF(chk = 0, uniq,
      LET(
         all_num, AND(ISNUMBER(filtArr)),
         all_int, IF(all_num, AND(filtArr = INT(filtArr)), FALSE),
         span_ok, IF(all_int, MAX(filtArr) - MIN(filtArr) + 1 = rowCount, FALSE),
         IF(AND(uniq = 1, all_int, span_ok), 1, 0)))
))

=== DESCRIPTION ===
Tests whether the values in an array are all different - and, optionally, whether they are a run of consecutive integers.

Arguments:
  arr      - the values to test (any shape; blanks and "" are ignored). Text comparison is case-insensitive ("A" and "a" count as the same value).
  [consec] - 0 (default): return 1 when all values are distinct, else 0.
             1: return 1 only when the values are distinct AND all integers AND consecutive (max - min + 1 = count), in any order; otherwise 0. A single integer counts as consecutive.

How consec works: once uniqueness is established, consecutive integers are exactly those whose range (max - min + 1) equals their count - {7;5;6} spans 5..7 with 3 values, so 1; {1;2;4} spans 1..4 with 3 values, so 0. Non-integers or text give 0 in consec mode.

Returns:
  1 or 0.

Example:
  is_unique({1;2;3})          ->  1
  is_unique({1;2;2})          ->  0
  is_unique({7;5;6}, 1)       ->  1     (consecutive in any order)
  is_unique({1;2;4}, 1)       ->  0     (gap)
  is_unique({1;2;2}, 1)       ->  0     (duplicate)
  is_unique({"a";"b"}, 1)     ->  0     (not integers)
